ew: China represented US$4.770 billion, or 49.9%, of Vale’s Q3 revenue destination, down 19% year over year; Q3 iron-ore sales to China were 50.290 Mt, down 4%. Iron-ore demand is linked to steel production and purchasing, and the meeting discussion also identified mill margins, inventories, alternative supply and scrap as factors to examine. On the call, Vale management forecast global crude-steel production of 1.9 billion tonnes in 2024, with China above 1 billion tonnes, and expected similar figures for 2025. Management expected Chinese stimulus to help stabilize steel consumption and thought manufacturing and infrastructure investment could offset weaker property-sector fixed-asset investment. It described the overall supply balance and inventories as stable, while noting high port inventories and low steel-mill inventories. Management said more than 50 Mt of supply had cost-curve support around US$95/t and nearly 150 Mt around US$90/t; it also cited blast-furnace utilization at 87%, improving steel margins (rebar about US$20/t higher and HRC around breakeven), and possible marginal growth outside China if Chinese exports declined. These are management’s dated outlook and estimates, not independently established present-day conditions. Management also said steel margins were then low and that iron-ore premiums could have upside if conditions improved.\n\nPortfolio, projects and strategy: In the October 25 transcript, Pimenta described a planned structural iron-ore production level of about 350 Mt, with 80–90% high-quality products, and said the aim was flexibility and value maximization rather than volume alone. The October 24 release cited a 2026 iron-ore production guidance range of 340–360 Mt. Vale said it had raised its 2024 production guidance to 323–330 Mt and, on the call, management expected the top end. Vargem Grande 1 wet processing began commissioning in September, one month ahead of schedule; the release described about 15 Mtpy of resumed capacity an